#!/bin/sh
# A stand-in `ssh` that records the exec argv it was given -- one token per line,
# in `argv.txt` beside itself -- and then does whatever the case staged in
# `tail.sh` beside it: `exec cat` to block like a live pane, or a listener to
# stand in for `ssh -L`.
#
# ## Why the varying half is SOURCED rather than written into this file
#
# Register item 467. The suite used to compose this script per case and the
# DAEMON then exec'd it, which is `ETXTBSY` waiting to happen: the kernel
# refuses to execute a file any process holds open for writing, and the harness
# runs its cases on THREADS of one process, so a sibling forking to spawn a
# program inherits the write handle and holds it until its own exec. Item 465
# measured that shape on `sprag-gate` at 10 failures in 30 runs.
#
# A tracked file cannot carry a per-case tail, so the tail is a DATA file the
# case writes next to this one. Nothing execs `tail.sh` -- `.` reads it -- so it
# carries none of the window this file was moved out of.
#
# `$0` is the path the kernel was handed, which is this file's link inside the
# case's own directory: that is what makes `argv.txt` and `tail.sh` findable
# without the suite substituting an absolute path into these bytes.
here=$(dirname "$0")
printf '%s\n' "$@" > "$here/argv.txt"
. "$here/tail.sh"
